    <body>
      <sec id="article-155299.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Myocardial viability refers to dysfunctional myocardium that, despite acute or chronic ischemia, retains the capacity to recover systolic function following revascularization. Ischemic injury produces both irreversible damage and adaptive states such as hibernating or stunned myocardium, in which contractile dysfunction persists in a low-perfusion environment yet remains potentially reversible. Assessment of myocardial viability is critical in chronic ischemic cardiomyopathy, chronic total coronary occlusion, and late-presenting ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction to guide revascularization decisions. Left ventricular ejection fraction serves as a key prognostic indicator in this population. This activity equips the participant with foundational knowledge of viability assessment techniques, interpretation of imaging findings, and clinical decision-making to optimize outcomes. Collaboration among an interprofessional team, including cardiology, radiology, and nursing, enhances diagnostic accuracy, coordinates timely interventions, and improves functional recovery and survival.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical features and imaging findings that suggest viable myocardium in patients with ischemic cardiomyopathy and heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret findings from dobutamine stress echocardiography, radionuclide imaging, positron emission tomography, and cardiac magnetic resonance when assessing myocardial viability.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based medical therapy for patients with ischemic cardiomyopathy and heart failure with reduced ejection fraction regardless of viability status.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with cardiologists, imaging specialists, surgeons, pharmacists, nurses, and other members of the interdisciplinary team to coordinate patient-centered care for ischemic cardiomyopathy.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=155299&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=155299">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Ischemic heart disease is the most common cause of chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF), and the prevalence of patients with ischemic cardiomyopathy is increasing in developed nations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r2">[2]</xref> A myocardium that is dysfunctional because of ischemia but potentially salvageable with revascularization is viable; viable myocardium can be stunned or hibernating. Evidence suggests that this state reflects an adaptive myocardial response to ischemia, with downregulation of metabolism and contractility. Myocardial viability assessment is essential for identifying patients with HFrEF who would benefit from revascularization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The blood supply to the epicardium, myocardium, and endocardium comes from the epicardial coronary arteries, and atherosclerotic coronary artery disease is the most common cause of myocardial ischemia, leading&#x000a0;to myocardial necrosis and ischemic cardiomyopathy over time. Repetitive ischemia of short duration causes a temporary loss of contractility, with potential recovery of function in viable myocardium within days to weeks, a condition&#x000a0;called myocardial stunning. Myocardial stunning refers to transient postischemic ventricular dysfunction that persists after blood flow is restored, despite the absence of irreversible damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Myocardial hibernation is an adaptation to chronic ischemia, with only minimal cell death, in which myocardial segments supplied by a coronary artery with fixed stenosis remain noncontractile due to downregulation of cellular metabolism but retain the potential to partially or fully recover upon revascularization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r5">[5]</xref> Myocardial hibernation represents a state of chronic left ventricular systolic dysfunction resulting from the adaptation of ischemic myocardium to critically reduced perfusion, thus establishing a new balance between reduced flow and function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r5">[5]</xref> Therefore, identifying hibernating myocardium in patients with HFrEF by various imaging modalities is important for selecting appropriate patients for revascularization and potentially improving left ventricular systolic function.</p>
        <p>However, whether assessing myocardial viability, in addition to guideline-directed medical therapy, in HFrEF translates into better clinical outcomes remains debatable. The landmark Surgical Treatment for Ischemic Heart Failure trial randomized patients with an ejection fraction of less than 35% to optimal medical therapy only or optimal medical therapy plus coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG). No significant difference in the primary endpoint, the rate of death from any cause, was observed between the 2 arms. However, patients in the optimal medical therapy plus CABG arm had lower rates of death from cardiovascular causes or hospitalization for cardiac causes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r6">[6]</xref> A substudy of the trial that studied patients who underwent single-photon emission computed tomography or dobutamine stress echocardiography for viability assessment showed that this benefit was observed regardless of myocardial viability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r7">[7]</xref> Results from this substudy additionally showed that viable myocardium was associated with improved ejection fraction regardless of CABG or medical therapy, highlighting the importance of optimal medical therapy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Causes of Myocardial Hibernation</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe coronary artery disease:&#x000a0;</bold>Multivessel disease or chronic total occlusion of an artery can cause myocardial hibernation.&#x000a0;Chronic reversible ischemia due to obstructive coronary artery disease leads to a reduction in resting blood flow and subsequent downregulation in metabolism and contractility. This myocardium is viable and recovers partially or fully upon revascularization.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Chronic myocardial ischemia:</bold>&#x000a0;This is due to a persistent demand-supply mismatch.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Repetitive myocardial stunning</bold> can&#x000a0;result in myocardial hibernation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Collateral-dependent circulation:</bold> Regions supplied mainly by collateral vessels may develop chronic hypoperfusion.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Causes of Myocardial Stunning</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Transient myocardial ischemia and reperfusion after acute ischemia:</bold>&#x000a0;An acute coronary syndrome due to plaque rupture leads to an ischemic cascade downstream and subsequent impairment in left ventricular contractility. The contractile dysfunction persists despite early revascularization and before irreversible myocardial injury sets in. The duration of transient post-ischemic dysfunction or stunning is a function of the duration of ischemia.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Coronary artery vasospasm</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>During percutaneous coronary intervention or balloon angioplasty</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Stress-related</bold>
<bold>myocardial dysfunction</bold></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>After cardiac surgical procedures:</bold> On-pump procedures, such as CABG, involve cardiopulmonary bypass, aortic cross-clamping, and cardioplegia, which interrupt coronary artery blood flow. This leads to ischemia, initially followed by reperfusion injury once the heart is restarted, resulting in reversible postischemic systolic dysfunction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r8">[8]</xref> This dysfunction may require vasopressor or inotropic support and mechanical circulatory support devices, such as extracorporeal membrane oxygenation, thereby prolonging the hospital stay.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Left atrial stunning:</bold>&#x000a0;This includes conversion of atrial fibrillation or flutter to sinus rhythm by electrical or pharmacological cardioversion, resulting in transient contractile dysfunction of the left atrium and left atrial appendage lasting about 4&#x000a0;to 6 weeks. A stunned atrium and appendage can serve as a nidus for thrombus formation. Hence, patients should take anticoagulation for at least 4 weeks</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Resuscitated cardiac arrest:</bold>&#x000a0;Myocardial dysfunction may result after the return of spontaneous circulation following a cardiac arrest.&#x000a0;This dysfunction manifests as global myocardial hypokinesis with a severe reduction in ejection fraction, reduction in cardiac output, and hypotension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r9">[9]</xref> Results from studies showed that myocardial blood flow is not reduced during the period of dysfunction, and myocardial recovery occurs after about 72 hours in survivors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>In developed countries, ischemic cardiomyopathy due to atherosclerotic coronary artery disease accounts for about 70% of patients with HFrEF. Results from studies showed that 50% of patients with coronary artery disease and heart failure with reduced ejection fraction have a significant volume of viable myocardium.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r11">[11]</xref> The overall mortality rate among patients with HFrEF due to ischemic cardiomyopathy is 39%, which is lower in patients with viable myocardium than in those with nonviable myocardium (37% vs 51%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Atherosclerosis leads to the accumulation of lipid-rich plaques in the coronary artery intima.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r13">[13]</xref> Plaques with thin fibrous caps, increased macrophage activity, large, lipid-rich necrotic cores, and superficial microcalcifications are considered vulnerable because they are easily eroded by exogenous shear stress, leading to plaque fissuring, rupture, destabilization, and exposure of the underlying thrombogenic, proinflammatory necrotic core. This process initiates the coagulation cascade and superimposed thrombosis, leading to downstream myocardial ischemia, fatal myocardial infarction, and sudden cardiac death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Myocardial ischemia follows a temporal sequence of events known as the ischemic cascade, which depends on the duration of ischemia. The ischemic cascade begins with a reduction in perfusion, leading to flow disparity and impairment of the sodium/potassium adenosine triphosphate pump, followed by diastolic dysfunction, systolic dysfunction, electrocardiographic changes, and angina. The extent of myocardial ischemia is a function of the duration of obstruction and the area of myocardium subtended by the diseased vessel.</p>
        <p>Partial occlusion for more than 5 hours causes patchy infarction, whereas complete occlusion for more than 20 minutes causes irreversible myocyte death, termed transmural infarction. The cascade of irreversible myocardial injury begins in the endocardium and then progresses to the myocardium. If reperfusion is established before the onset of irreversible damage after myocardial infarction, stunning develops, and the time required for recovery depends on the duration of ischemia.&#x000a0;In chronic stunning, oxygen-free radicals released during interrupted blood flow damage myocytes and cause myocardial stunning. The sequential evolution of myocardial injury following ischemia depends on ischemia duration, residual collateral blood flow, the location of the occlusion, and myocardial oxygen consumption.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Ischemic preconditioning and postconditioning are the adaptive responses believed to reduce infarct size. Brief reversible ischemic episodes preceding the major ischemic event are cardioprotective because they reduce the extent of myocardial necrosis. This phenomenon is called ischemic preconditioning; preconditioned myocytes can tolerate ischemia better than naive myocytes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Myocardial postconditioning occurs when intermittent episodes of ischemia and reperfusion during reperfusion therapy are believed to be cardioprotective by reducing reperfusion injury and infarct size. Complex endogenous second-messenger systems, such as protein kinase C, inducible nitric oxide synthase, cyclooxygenase-2, and mitochondrial potassium channels,&#x000a0;are believed to be active.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from studies suggested that myocardial hibernation and stress-induced ischemia without angina are common in patients with HFrEF, and revascularization may improve resting blood flow and left ventricular contractility.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r16">[16]</xref> Therefore, both chronic stunning and hibernation present with reversible systolic dysfunction, but resting blood flow is normal in myocardial stunning, whereas it is critically reduced in hibernation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The resting myocardium uses &#x003b2;-oxidation of fatty acids as a metabolic fuel to produce high-energy phosphates like adenosinetriphosphate to power myocardial contraction. However, the ischemic myocardium shifts its energy metabolism from fatty acid oxidation to anaerobic glycolysis. Consequently, glucose uptake is upregulated in ischemic and viable myocardium, and clinicians use this phenomenon to assess myocardial viability with 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission tomography.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Histologically, cardiac myocytes in human hibernating myocardium are depleted of their contractile material and filled with glycogen.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Compared to infarcted myocardium, viable myocardium has been shown to have normal microvascular resistance, and the degree of microvascular dysfunction after acute myocardial infarction is an important negative predictor of myocardial recovery after revascularization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Patients with left ventricular dysfunction may be asymptomatic or may present with exertional fatigue, palpitations, angina, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, or orthopnea. Sudden cardiac arrest or sudden cardiac death may be the initial presentation. General physical examination findings vary with the stage of the disease and may include respiratory distress, cyanosis, elevated jugular venous pressure, bilateral pitting pedal edema, or congestive hepatomegaly. The precordial examination may reveal a displaced apex beat or a pansystolic murmur of secondary mitral regurgitation<bold>.</bold></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The physiology of the ischemic cascade is fundamental to stress testing and myocardial viability assessment, as different imaging modalities target distinct stages of the cascade. The following imaging modalities are commonly used to evaluate myocardial viability.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Dobutamine Stress Echocardiography</bold>
</p>
        <p>Dobutamine stress echocardiography is used to assess myocardial viability in patients with resting systolic dysfunction before revascularization. This technique is based on the principle that viable myocardium will increase contractility in response to &#x003b2;-adrenergic stimulation, whereas nonviable myocardium will not. Dobutamine is infused at a rate of 2.5 to 10 &#x000b5;g/kg/min.</p>
        <p>Improvement in contractility at low dose followed by diminished wall motion at high dose (10 to 40 &#x000b5;g/kg/min) is termed a biphasic response and suggests viable myocardium. A biphasic response predicts recovery of myocardial contractility after revascularization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r20">[20]</xref> This phenomenon is explained by an increase in myocardial flow and contractility at low doses, but by a significant myocardial oxygen demand-supply mismatch during high-dose dobutamine infusion.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Radionuclide Techniques</bold>
</p>
        <p>Radiotracers used in the viability study are analogs of endogenous second messengers and metabolites involved&#x000a0;in cell signaling and metabolism. Quantitative analysis of these radiotracers' uptake thus yields indirect evidence of intact cellular function and the integrity of the cell membrane and subcellular organelles. These tracers have unstable nuclei and electrons that emit characteristic radiation after being taken up by myocardial cells, and cameras convert this radiation into photons for imaging.</p>
        <p>The degree of uptake is proportional to myocardial blood flow. The strength of the emitted radiation correlates with the degree of uptake and, thus, the extent of viability. Infarcted myocardium does not take up tracers; hence, no radiation is emitted by this portion of the myocardium. The images show perfusion defects in that area. A radiotracer perfusion study thus aims to assess the flow-disparity component of the ischemic cascade, which is its early stage. Areas with normal perfusion are viable, but areas with perfusion defects may be infarcted or hibernating. Metabolic tracers like 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose (18F-FDG), as well as delayed imaging with thallium-201, help differentiate this. The following imaging protocols are commonly used for viability assessment.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Thallium-201 single-photon emission computed tomography study</bold>
</p>
        <p>Thallium-201 is a potassium analog, and its uptake via Na/K (sodium/potassium) adenosine triphosphatease is evidence of an intact cell membrane and, thus, cellular viability. Thallium-201 has a high first-pass myocardial extraction of about 85% after intravenous injection. The initial myocardial uptake after intravenous injection is a function of blood flow, and hence, uptake will be high in normal myocardium and low in ischemic myocardium.</p>
        <p>Thallium has a unique property of late redistribution from the myocardium to the blood pool, which depends on the concentration difference between the myocardium and blood pool, and not on blood flow. Since the concentration difference between the blood pool and ischemic myocardium is low, washout is low, retaining more thallium-201 activity, while washout is high in normal myocardium. This phenomenon of differential washout is used in the viability study.</p>
        <p>The rest or stress images are obtained 15 to 20 minutes after injection, and the redistribution images are obtained 3 to 4 hours later. The presence of a perfusion defect on initial images, followed by improvement on redistribution images, is the hallmark of myocardial viability. The specificity of this study can be further improved by enhancing redistribution through reinjection of the tracer during the redistribution stage, or by obtaining late distribution images at 24 to 48 hours, thereby allowing more time for redistribution and improving the positive predictive value for improvement in left ventricular function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Technetium-99m sestamibi single-photon emission computed tomography</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Tc-99m sestamibi is a cation that diffuses across the cell membrane and then into the mitochondria, and positive uptake is suggestive of viable myocardial cells. The protocols used are the rest-stress protocol and the rest protocol with nitrate enhancement to promote uptake.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Normal uptake is consistent with viable myocardium, moderate uptake is consistent with a mixture of infarct and viable tissue, and absent uptake is consistent with an infarct.</p>
        <p>
<bold>18F-FDG positron emission tomography scan</bold>
</p>
        <p>18F-FDG is a glucose analog labeled with a radiotracer that is taken up by mitochondria, phosphorylated during glycolysis, and trapped in this state without further metabolism. In hibernating myocardium, glucose transporter receptors are overexpressed. Therefore, the myocardium is FDG-avid.</p>
        <p>In ischemic myocardium, hypoxia suppresses fatty acid &#x003b2;-oxidation, prompting cells to switch to anaerobic glycolysis for energy production. Viable tissue characteristically shows flow-metabolism mismatch. The first component assesses perfusion with positron emission tomography (PET) radiotracers such as rubidium-82 and <sup>13</sup>N-labeled ammonia, and hibernating myocardium exhibits perfusion defects.</p>
        <p>The metabolic component of the test consists of keeping the patient fasting for 6 hours, followed by a glucose load, an insulin infusion, and, finally, an 18F-FDG radiotracer. Insulin facilitates uptake in hibernating and normal myocardium, and the PET scanner shows uniform uptake.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;The absence of uptake is consistent with an infarct.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Contrast-Enhanced Cardiac Magnetic Resonance&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The initial method of viability assessment using cardiac magnetic resonance is quantifying end-diastolic wall thickness. A myocardial thickness of less than 6 mm is likely scar tissue and is less likely to improve after revascularization. One of the fundamental histologic changes in necrotic myocardium is extracellular volume expansion, along with myocyte death. This change enables high uptake and retention of high-density gadolinium-based contrast agents in the cell and extracellular space, a phenomenon termed late gadolinium enhancement (LGE), and forms the basis for a viability study using contrast-enhanced cardiac magnetic resonance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>LGE starts from the endocardium (subendocardial infarct) and then progresses transmurally (transmural infarct) in the segments supplied by the coronary artery. The degree of LGE correlates with the extent of viability and potential benefit from revascularization. A myocardial segment with greater than 50% LGE is nonviable and will not improve after revascularization, a segment with less than 25% LGE is viable and will benefit from revascularization, and 25% to 50% LGE needs to be further investigated for inotropic contractile reserve by dobutamine infusion and assessment of wall motion by echocardiography or cardiac magnetic resonance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>A transmural LGE in the right coronary artery consistent with transmural infarction and lack of viability (see <bold>Image.</bold> Late Gadolinium Enhanced Cardiac MRI).&#x000a0;Dobutamine stress cardiac magnetic resonance can be used to assess viability. A dysfunctional but viable myocardium will exhibit an increased systolic thickening of greater than 2 mm on low-dose dobutamine stress.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Identifying patients with ischemic cardiomyopathy who would benefit from revascularization in addition to guideline-directed medical therapy is important. All patients with HFrEF should receive optimal medical therapy, irrespective of the presence or absence of viable myocardium.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>&#x003b2;-blockers</bold>
</p>
        <p>Selected &#x000df;-blockers, carvedilol, metoprolol, and bisoprolol, in maximum tolerated doses, have proven mortality and morbidity benefits in patients with HFrEF (American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association [ACC/AHA] Class 1A).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors/angiotensin receptor blockers/angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitors</bold>
</p>
        <p>Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system inhibitors, particularly enalapril, and angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitors, such as sacubitril-valsartan, also have a mortality benefit (ACC/AHA Class 1A). Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitors should not be used together, and a washout period of at least 36 hours should be given before transitioning from an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor to an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ACC/AHA Class 1A).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists</bold>
</p>
        <p>Spironolactone and eplerenone are proven to reduce mortality and morbidity and are recommended with a level of recommendation Class 1A by the ACC/AHA for New York Heart Association (NYHA) Class 2 to 4 heart failure and left ventricular ejection fraction less than 35% (ACC/AHA Class 1A).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hydralazine and isosorbide dinitrate</bold>
</p>
        <p>The combination reduces mortality and morbidity and is used predominantly in Black patients with NYHA class 3 to 4 HFrEF despite receiving &#x003b2;-blockers and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (Class 1A). These drugs can be used as alternative preload- and afterload-reducing agents in patients who cannot receive angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, or angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitors due to hypersensitivity, hypotension, and renal failure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitors:</bold>
<bold>Sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitors</bold> (<bold>empagliflozin and dapagliflozin</bold>)</p>
        <p>These medications reduce heart failure-related hospitalization and cardiovascular mortality in patients with symptomatic HFrEF irrespective of the presence of type 2 diabetes mellitus (ACC/AHA Class 1).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Coronary Artery Revascularization</bold>
</p>
        <p>Coronary artery bypass grafting is performed for left main disease, triple-vessel disease, complex coronary artery disease, and patients with coronary artery disease and reduced left ventricular ejection fraction. Percutaneous coronary intervention with balloon angioplasty and stent placement is done for uncomplicated single- or 2-vessel disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Device-Based Therapies for Cardiomyopathy-Related Arrhythmias</bold>
</p>
        <p>An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is used in patients with end-stage cardiomyopathy with nonviable myocardium to prevent sudden cardiac arrest from malignant ventricular arrhythmias. Cardiac resynchronization therapy synchronizes left ventricular and right ventricular contraction in HFrEF, prevents progression of heart failure, and improves symptoms. An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is indicated for the primary prevention of sudden cardiac death in patients with left ventricular ejection fraction less than 35%,&#x000a0;NYHA class 2 or 3 heart failure who have received maximally tolerated doses of a &#x003b2;-blocker, an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor, and a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist for 90 days, and with survival expected to exceed 1 year. Patients with left bundle branch block and a QRS complex duration of 150 ms or more, and NYHA Class 2 or 3 symptoms despite guideline-directed medical therapy,&#x000a0;benefit from upgrading to a cardiac resynchronization device.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis includes:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Stress-induced cardiomyopathy:&#x000a0;</bold>Any form of myocardial stress, like sepsis and emotional and physical trauma, can lead to a catecholaminergic surge, microvascular spasm, and myocardial dysfunction. This condition is characterized by reversible distal myocardial hypocontractility with basal hypercontractility on echocardiography. The&#x000a0;clinical presentation is similar to acute coronary syndrome, and it is a diagnosis of exclusion. Obstructive coronary artery disease needs to be ruled out by cardiac catheterization.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Left ventricular</bold>
<bold>systolic dysfunction</bold>
<bold>due to myocarditis:</bold>&#x000a0;Viruses such as coxsackievirus and parvovirus B19 can cause transient,&#x000a0;reversible myocardial dysfunction.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy:&#x000a0;</bold>Any tachycardia, such as atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular response, supraventricular tachycardia, or atrial flutter with a rapid ventricular response, can cause reversible systolic myocardial dysfunction. Myocardial function recovers after rate control.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Peripartum cardiomyopathy: </bold>Peripartum cardiomyopathy is defined by the development of congestive heart failure in the last month of pregnancy or within 5 weeks of puerperium. This disorder is thought to be due to the hemodynamic stress of pregnancy on the heart. Left ventricular systolic function often improves with time.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s10" sec-type="Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials">
        <title>Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials</title>
        <p>Results from extensive landmark studies assessing the mortality benefits of revascularization in viable myocardium are not yet available, and ongoing studies are underway. Small- and moderate-sized studies showed no mortality benefit from revascularization and medical therapy versus medical therapy only in patients with viable myocardium. However, these studies were statistically underpowered and confounded by many factors.</p>
        <p>In a subanalysis of the Surgical Treatment for Ischemic Heart Failure (STICH) trial, 601 patients underwent a viability study before revascularization; about half received optimal medical therapy with CABG, and the other half received optimal medical therapy only. The arm with CABG and medical therapy had lower mortality, but the difference was not statistically significant.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Findings from the STICH trial additionally showed that the increase in ejection fraction was greater in patients with viable myocardium. However, this increase was similar in both the CABG with medical therapy and the medical therapy-only groups. Results from the FDG-PET Assisted Management of Patients With Severe Left Ventricular Dysfunction and Suspected Coronary Disease: A Randomized, Controlled Trial (PARR-2) study showed that PET viability imaging did not demonstrate survival benefits with revascularization compared to medical therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The most important benefit of revascularization may be improved ejection fraction and a reduction in future coronary events, because the patients who benefited most in the STICH trial were those with multivessel disease and severely reduced ejection fraction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r22">[22]</xref> The benefits of CABG can be extrapolated from survival benefits to improvements in quality of life and exercise tolerance in patients with ischemic cardiomyopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r23">[23]</xref> The revascularized myocardium is electrically stable and less likely to be a source of malignant ventricular arrhythmias.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Diagnosing hibernating myocardium in patients with ischemic cardiomyopathy is essential because symptoms of chronic left ventricular dysfunction may not always result from necrotic and scarred myocardium from previous myocardial infarctions but rather from reversible ischemia. Hibernating myocardium may be the predominant etiology of chronic heart failure in patients with a history of known or suspected coronary artery disease, and the degree of cardiac dysfunction may not be solely accounted for by previous myocardial infarctions.</p>
        <p>The prognosis of ischemic cardiomyopathy with scarred myocardium due to previous myocardial infarctions is dismal, and ventricular assist devices and cardiac transplant may be the only alternatives ultimately.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-155299.r12">[12]</xref> However, the prognosis of patients with hibernating myocardium presenting as chronic heart failure may be better with revascularization. The ACC/AHA recommends myocardial viability testing in chronic ischemic cardiomyopathy with a Class 2a level of recommendation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Untreated myocardial dysfunction may lead to:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>End-stage heart failure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Multiorgan failure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Heart failure hospitalization</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Arrhythmia hospitalization</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sudden cardiac death</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Need for device-based therapy or left ventricular assist devices</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Need for cardiac transplant</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>The importance of adherence to medical therapy should be discussed with patients with HFrEF, as these medications have been shown to halt the progression of cardiomyopathy, improve ejection fraction, and reduce mortality and morbidity. Regular follow-up to ensure optimal fluid volume status, electrolyte balance, and renal function is important because these medications frequently cause renal injury and hyperkalemia. Revascularization is likely to improve the patient's cardiac function if viable myocardium is present.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Managing chronic heart failure requires close interdisciplinary collaboration among emergency department clinicians, hospitalists, general cardiologists, advanced cardiovascular imaging specialists, nuclear cardiologists, cardiac electrophysiologists, advanced practice registered nurses, radiologists, interventional cardiologists, cardiothoracic surgeons, and nurses. Patients with acute decompensation generally present to the emergency department and are initially seen by emergency department clinicians and then admitted to the hospital ward under a hospitalist or to the coronary care unit under a general cardiologist, where they are started on appropriate medications, including diuretics, inotropes, and vasopressors. Advanced cardiovascular imaging specialists, radiologists, and nuclear cardiologists are responsible for interpreting cardiac imaging and viability studies.</p>
        <p>If substantial viability is present, an interventional cardiologist or a cardiothoracic surgeon should be consulted for percutaneous intervention or CABG. A general cardiologist treats the patient in the clinic, and cardiac electrophysiologists treat the patient in the device clinic. When medical therapy is used, the pharmacist should provide input on the selected drugs, verify dosing, perform medication reconciliation, and counsel patients on their medications.</p>
        <p>Nurses are essential in administering medications to patients, monitoring telemetry, vital signs, and fluid status while patients are hospitalized, counseling about dietary and lifestyle modifications, treating patients in heart failure clinics, and monitoring them during cardiac rehabilitation sessions. Nurses must be empowered to bring any concerns to the attention of the clinicians, and their suggestions must be received as those of a colleague. This type of open communication model is crucial for interprofessional care to succeed.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-155299.s15">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
